1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,804
Somebody, fifty thousand years ago, drilled a hole through a bird bone the width of a hair.

2
00:00:05,804 --> 00:00:06,674
Not for food.

3
00:00:06,674 --> 00:00:07,797
Not for a weapon.

4
00:00:07,797 --> 00:00:08,541
For a seam.

5
00:00:08,541 --> 00:00:09,538
A cold morning.

6
00:00:09,538 --> 00:00:10,851
You own one garment.

7
00:00:10,851 --> 00:00:11,659
It's a hide.

8
00:00:11,659 --> 00:00:15,059
Not a coat — a coat implies somebody made a decision.

9
00:00:15,059 --> 00:00:22,127
This is a hide, folded over one shoulder, held shut with your hand, because your other hand is busy being cold.

10
00:00:22,127 --> 00:00:24,579
And that outfit is genuinely terrible.

11
00:00:24,579 --> 00:00:26,271
It hangs open at the neck.

12
00:00:26,271 --> 00:00:28,660
It sets like cardboard when it dries.

13
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:34,400
It won't hold your wrist or your ankle, which is exactly where heat leaves a body fastest.

14
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:37,927
So the question isn't how somebody wore an animal skin.

15
00:00:37,927 --> 00:00:39,935
Anyone can wear an animal skin.

16
00:00:39,935 --> 00:00:45,360
It's when clothing stopped being something you drape, and became something you build.

17
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:46,167
Three finds.

18
00:00:46,167 --> 00:00:50,136
A cave in Morocco full of tools nobody would photograph twice.

19
00:00:50,136 --> 00:00:50,943
That needle.

20
00:00:50,943 --> 00:00:56,810
And a grave with three thousand beads, which is not a clothing story until, abruptly, it is.

21
00:00:56,810 --> 00:00:59,451
We don't have one garment from any of it.

22
00:00:59,451 --> 00:01:00,322
Not a sleeve.

23
00:01:00,322 --> 00:01:01,192
Not a stitch.

24
00:01:01,192 --> 00:01:03,960
We have the workshop, and we have the mess.

25
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:04,692
Clothes rot.

26
00:01:04,692 --> 00:01:05,825
That's the problem.

27
00:01:05,825 --> 00:01:07,303
That's the whole problem.

28
00:01:07,303 --> 00:01:13,598
Hide is skin — protein, water, and lunch for bacteria — and it vanishes long before the interesting rocks do.

29
00:01:13,598 --> 00:01:17,255
So for most of human prehistory, we aren't looking at clothing.

30
00:01:17,255 --> 00:01:19,249
We're looking at the shadow of it.

31
00:01:19,249 --> 00:01:21,128
A bone tool with a polish on it.

32
00:01:21,128 --> 00:01:23,236
A blade that cuts skin and not meat.

33
00:01:23,236 --> 00:01:27,352
A bead that had to be attached to something, and presumably not a rock.

34
00:01:27,352 --> 00:01:30,837
And then there's the evidence that isn't archaeology at all.

35
00:01:30,837 --> 00:01:31,454
It's lice.

36
00:01:31,454 --> 00:01:34,308
Head lice live on heads, on account of the heads.

37
00:01:34,308 --> 00:01:38,137
Body lice live in clothing, because they need fabric to hold onto.

38
00:01:38,137 --> 00:01:39,787
They evolved from head lice.

39
00:01:39,787 --> 00:01:49,351
So the moment those two stopped being the same animal is roughly the moment humans started wearing clothes often enough for a parasite to move in and get comfortable.

40
00:01:49,351 --> 00:01:56,105
In 2011, Melissa Toups, Andrew Kitchen and David Reed put that split at no less than eighty-three thousand years ago.

41
00:01:56,105 --> 00:01:58,443
Possibly a hundred and seventy thousand.

42
00:01:58,443 --> 00:02:00,092
That's a window, not a date.

43
00:02:00,092 --> 00:02:01,513
Windows are what we get.

44
00:02:01,513 --> 00:02:09,758
But it drops the habit deep into the African Middle Stone Age, tens of thousands of years before anyone needed clothes to get through a winter.

45
00:02:09,758 --> 00:02:11,235
Which flips the question.

46
00:02:11,235 --> 00:02:13,516
Not, did they wear clothes — obviously.

47
00:02:13,516 --> 00:02:15,280
How well could they make them.

48
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:18,469
Contrebandiers Cave, on the Atlantic coast of Morocco.

49
00:02:18,469 --> 00:02:21,600
Close enough to the sea that you can hear it working.

50
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:31,253
Emily Hallett and her colleagues went through more than twelve thousand bone fragments, from layers dated between a hundred and twenty and ninety thousand years old.

51
00:02:31,253 --> 00:02:33,860
Sixty-two of them had been shaped by people.

52
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:36,816
The one worth your attention looks like a spatula.

53
00:02:36,816 --> 00:02:44,490
It's a rib — antelope, or wild cattle — split and flattened, with an edge that's been dragged across wet hide so often it polished.

54
00:02:44,490 --> 00:02:45,641
You'd walk past it.

55
00:02:45,641 --> 00:02:47,665
Under a microscope it has grooves.

56
00:02:47,665 --> 00:02:50,097
Then there's the detail I can't get over.

57
00:02:50,097 --> 00:02:55,266
Hallett found cut marks on the paws and jaws of sand foxes, golden jackals and wildcats.

58
00:02:55,266 --> 00:02:56,534
Not butchering marks.

59
00:02:56,534 --> 00:02:57,452
Skinning marks.

60
00:02:57,452 --> 00:03:03,087
The animal had been opened at the feet, and the skin pulled up, in one piece, over its own head.

61
00:03:03,087 --> 00:03:03,830
Like a sock.

62
00:03:03,830 --> 00:03:06,204
Somebody took a wildcat off like a sock.

63
00:03:06,204 --> 00:03:07,879
That is not a hungry person.

64
00:03:07,879 --> 00:03:11,883
That's a person who has decided the fur is worth more than the meat.

65
00:03:11,883 --> 00:03:15,247
Now, careful — this is where documentaries start fibbing.

66
00:03:15,247 --> 00:03:18,262
It does not prove anyone in that cave sewed a coat.

67
00:03:18,262 --> 00:03:22,790
The researchers call it strong proxy evidence, and that's the honest version.

68
00:03:22,790 --> 00:03:29,357
What it does prove is a trade: an animal chosen for its pelt, taken whole, and a tool that made the result soft.

69
00:03:29,357 --> 00:03:31,731
A hundred and twenty thousand years ago.

70
00:03:31,731 --> 00:03:34,920
Before our species had got anywhere near cold country.

71
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:36,973
If you want to make clothing out of hide, cutting is easy.

72
00:03:36,973 --> 00:03:37,874
Joining is the nightmare.

73
00:03:37,874 --> 00:03:38,599
You can fold a hide.

74
00:03:38,599 --> 00:03:39,989
You can tie it with a strip of leather.

75
00:03:39,989 --> 00:03:40,190
Fine.

76
00:03:40,190 --> 00:03:47,625
But the second you want a sleeve, or a seam that holds when you pull it, you have to get thread through the material — and the tool has to make a hole no bigger than the thread, or you have built a cold-air vent.

77
00:03:47,625 --> 00:03:48,665
The first answer was the awl.

78
00:03:48,665 --> 00:03:49,285
A sharpened bone.

79
00:03:49,285 --> 00:03:50,710
Push it through, open a hole, thread it.

80
00:03:50,710 --> 00:03:51,051
It works.

81
00:03:51,051 --> 00:03:51,637
It leaves a gap.

82
00:03:51,637 --> 00:03:56,835
And every puncture quietly weakens the hide, so your beautiful new garment is now a perforated hide, which is the ancient equivalent of a paper bag.

83
00:03:56,835 --> 00:03:59,308
A needle fixes that, because the needle carries the thread in with it.

84
00:03:59,308 --> 00:04:02,794
The oldest bone needle anyone cites comes from Denisova Cave, in the mountains of southern Siberia.

85
00:04:02,794 --> 00:04:05,161
Seven centimetres of bird bone, with an eye worked through the end.

86
00:04:05,161 --> 00:04:06,306
Around fifty thousand years old.

87
00:04:06,306 --> 00:04:09,408
And here's what should stop you: it came out of layers that also held Denisovan remains.

88
00:04:09,408 --> 00:04:10,797
It also can't be assigned to a species.

89
00:04:10,797 --> 00:04:12,327
Anyone who tells you otherwise is guessing.

90
00:04:12,327 --> 00:04:16,162
Fifty thousand years ago, somebody sat down and drilled a hole through a sliver of bone without splitting it.

91
00:04:16,162 --> 00:04:17,377
That is not a caveman with a rock.

92
00:04:17,377 --> 00:04:19,046
That's a specialist with a bad afternoon ahead.

93
00:04:19,046 --> 00:04:19,806
Then it gets strange.

94
00:04:19,806 --> 00:04:21,161
Eyed needles don't turn up everywhere.

95
00:04:21,161 --> 00:04:26,324
They turn up in the cold parts of Eurasia, around forty thousand years ago, right as the climate starts collapsing toward the last glacial maximum.

96
00:04:26,324 --> 00:04:27,120
They already had awls.

97
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,020
They already had clothes.

98
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:28,990
So why build the hard tool?

99
00:04:28,990 --> 00:04:34,599
In 2024, Ian Gilligan and colleagues argued the answer isn't survival in the boring sense.

100
00:04:34,599 --> 00:04:37,180
A scraped hide gives you a loose garment.

101
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:39,143
Good enough for a cool evening.

102
00:04:39,143 --> 00:04:49,017
Useless in a glacial winter, where what keeps you alive isn't one thick layer but several thin ones — and the one against your skin is the one that has to fit.

103
00:04:49,017 --> 00:04:50,609
Fitted means fine sewing.

104
00:04:50,609 --> 00:04:52,696
Fine sewing means an eyed needle.

105
00:04:52,696 --> 00:04:58,676
Gilligan's summary of the entire innovation, and I love him for this, is essentially: underwear.

106
00:04:58,676 --> 00:05:02,369
But the argument keeps going, and it goes somewhere better.

107
00:05:02,369 --> 00:05:06,371
Think about how you announce yourself when your skin is showing.

108
00:05:06,371 --> 00:05:06,789
Ochre.

109
00:05:06,789 --> 00:05:07,207
Scars.

110
00:05:07,207 --> 00:05:07,625
Paint.

111
00:05:07,625 --> 00:05:10,145
Beads pressed right up against the body.

112
00:05:10,145 --> 00:05:14,889
Now wrap that body in hide, for months, because the alternative is freezing.

113
00:05:14,889 --> 00:05:18,644
Everything you used to say about yourself has nowhere to go.

114
00:05:18,644 --> 00:05:20,483
So you put it on the clothes.

115
00:05:20,483 --> 00:05:22,693
That's what the needle is for, too.

116
00:05:22,693 --> 00:05:23,111
Beads.

117
00:05:23,111 --> 00:05:23,591
Shells.

118
00:05:23,591 --> 00:05:24,504
Strips of fur.

119
00:05:24,504 --> 00:05:26,961
Fine enough to read from across a fire.

120
00:05:26,961 --> 00:05:30,531
Clothing stops being a thermostat and becomes a sentence.

121
00:05:30,531 --> 00:05:35,337
It's a hypothesis, built from timing and tools, and Gilligan says so himself.

122
00:05:35,337 --> 00:05:39,030
But once you know to look for it, you can't stop seeing it.

123
00:05:39,030 --> 00:05:44,209
In the foothills of the Caucasus, in Georgia, there's a cave called Dzudzuana.

124
00:05:44,209 --> 00:05:49,913
In 2009, a team led by Eliso Kvavadze and Ofer Bar-Yosef was not looking for clothing.

125
00:05:49,913 --> 00:05:58,379
They were studying ancient climate, which meant pollen, which meant dissolving cave dirt and counting grains under a microscope.

126
00:05:58,379 --> 00:05:59,416
Glamorous work.

127
00:05:59,416 --> 00:06:01,175
What they found was fibre.

128
00:06:01,175 --> 00:06:01,883
Wild flax.

129
00:06:01,883 --> 00:06:06,470
Thousands of strands, in layers up to thirty-four thousand years old.

130
00:06:06,470 --> 00:06:10,136
Some of it was twisted — spun, deliberately, into cord.

131
00:06:10,136 --> 00:06:11,304
Some was knotted.

132
00:06:11,304 --> 00:06:12,801
And some was coloured.

133
00:06:12,801 --> 00:06:14,363
Black, grey, turquoise.

134
00:06:14,363 --> 00:06:15,531
And one was pink.

135
00:06:15,531 --> 00:06:17,027
The flax grew outside.

136
00:06:17,027 --> 00:06:18,984
The colours came from plants.

137
00:06:18,984 --> 00:06:25,215
Which means someone took a plant, pulled the fibre out, spun it into thread, and then dyed it.

138
00:06:25,215 --> 00:06:25,857
For rope.

139
00:06:25,857 --> 00:06:26,696
For baskets.

140
00:06:26,696 --> 00:06:27,469
For sewing.

141
00:06:27,469 --> 00:06:31,201
We don't know which, and no textile survives to tell us.

142
00:06:31,201 --> 00:06:32,698
But here's why I care.

143
00:06:32,698 --> 00:06:35,115
Dyeing is not a survival technology.

144
00:06:35,115 --> 00:06:38,453
You do not colour a rope so the rope works better.

145
00:06:38,453 --> 00:06:48,365
Around the same era, at Pavlov in the Czech Republic, fired clay kept the impression of woven fibre — the negative of a textile that no longer exists.

146
00:06:48,365 --> 00:06:53,084
So this was never hides-or-cloth, with cloth arriving fashionably late.

147
00:06:53,084 --> 00:06:56,750
It was both, in the same toolkit, for a very long time.

148
00:06:56,750 --> 00:06:59,588
And now the part nobody puts in the documentary.

149
00:06:59,588 --> 00:07:00,680
We know the tools.

150
00:07:00,680 --> 00:07:01,772
We know the fibre.

151
00:07:01,772 --> 00:07:03,097
We know the cut marks.

152
00:07:03,097 --> 00:07:13,326
What we do not have is one complete garment older than about five thousand years — and that one survived because it was buried in Egypt, by accident, in the right kind of dry.

153
00:07:13,326 --> 00:07:19,307
We don't know whether a hide garment was a rectangle with a hole for the head, or a coat with sleeves.

154
00:07:19,307 --> 00:07:21,621
We don't know the colours anyone liked.

155
00:07:21,621 --> 00:07:29,115
Every museum figure standing there in a stitched, fitted, beautifully tailored prehistoric outfit is a guess wearing a lab coat.

156
00:07:29,115 --> 00:07:30,498
That's not a complaint.

157
00:07:30,498 --> 00:07:32,230
That's where the story lives.

158
00:07:32,230 --> 00:07:37,378
Two hundred kilometres east of Moscow, near Vladimir, there's a site called Sunghir.

159
00:07:37,378 --> 00:07:43,679
Around thirty-four thousand years ago, people buried their dead there, and they did not do it casually.

160
00:07:43,679 --> 00:07:49,313
The best-known grave holds a man, thirty-five to forty-five years old, covered in red ochre.

161
00:07:49,313 --> 00:07:50,634
And covered in beads.

162
00:07:50,634 --> 00:07:52,078
Three thousand of them.

163
00:07:52,078 --> 00:07:52,974
Mammoth ivory.

164
00:07:52,974 --> 00:07:53,992
Each one carved.

165
00:07:53,992 --> 00:07:55,071
Each one pierced.

166
00:07:55,071 --> 00:07:58,397
Each one small enough to make a modern jeweller swear.

167
00:07:58,397 --> 00:08:02,087
With him: ivory armbands, and hundreds of pierced fox teeth.

168
00:08:02,087 --> 00:08:08,085
And the beads were lying in rows, in the pattern of a garment, because they had been sewn onto it.

169
00:08:08,085 --> 00:08:14,326
Thirty-four thousand years ago, somebody drilled three thousand holes in ivory to decorate one outfit.

170
00:08:14,326 --> 00:08:17,834
That's the moment this stops being a story about weather.

171
00:08:17,834 --> 00:08:19,216
A hide keeps you warm.

172
00:08:19,216 --> 00:08:23,088
A hide with three thousand hand-cut beads on it says something.

173
00:08:23,088 --> 00:08:23,924
Who this was.

174
00:08:23,924 --> 00:08:26,461
What the community could afford to spend.

175
00:08:26,461 --> 00:08:28,754
What they thought the dead were owed.

176
00:08:28,754 --> 00:08:35,966
And in the same cemetery, two children were buried head to head, with ivory spears and discs and thousands more beads.

177
00:08:35,966 --> 00:08:40,082
Whatever those beads meant, they were not a reward for a long life.

178
00:08:40,082 --> 00:08:41,950
Those children hadn't had one.

179
00:08:41,950 --> 00:08:42,173
So.

180
00:08:42,173 --> 00:08:44,828
What did ancient humans wear before tailors?

181
00:08:44,828 --> 00:08:46,534
They were already tailoring.

182
00:08:46,534 --> 00:08:52,215
The tailor — the one who fits a garment to a body — is in the tools long before there's a shop.

183
00:08:52,215 --> 00:08:57,124
What changed across ninety thousand years isn't whether people could make clothes.

184
00:08:57,124 --> 00:08:58,771
It's what clothes were for.

185
00:08:58,771 --> 00:08:59,231
Warmth.

186
00:08:59,231 --> 00:08:59,810
Then fit.

187
00:08:59,810 --> 00:09:03,237
Then, once the body was covered all year round, identity.

188
00:09:03,237 --> 00:09:05,061
And almost none of it is left.

189
00:09:05,061 --> 00:09:10,149
The softest, most personal object in a human life is the first thing the ground eats.

190
00:09:10,149 --> 00:09:11,736
What we have is a scraper.

191
00:09:11,736 --> 00:09:14,213
A needle with a hole the width of a hair.

192
00:09:14,213 --> 00:09:19,419
And a few fibres that somebody dyed turquoise, for no reason except that they liked it.

193
00:09:19,419 --> 00:09:22,727
Next time you pull on a coat, notice what you're doing.

194
00:09:22,727 --> 00:09:28,526
You're putting on the only technology that has to be rebuilt for every single body that wears it.

195
00:09:28,526 --> 00:09:28,927
Right.

196
00:09:28,927 --> 00:09:31,048
Subscribe if you want more of this.

197
00:09:31,048 --> 00:09:34,118
And tell me — one hide, one bone needle, no thread.

198
00:09:34,118 --> 00:09:35,350
What are you making?
